<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <application>
        <!--
          The JavaScript engine runs in its own isolated process.

          android:process=":jsengine" is the only available way to STOP a runaway script. No
          Android QuickJS binding exposes JS_SetInterruptHandler, so a script that never yields
          — a plain `while (true) {}` — cannot be interrupted from inside the VM, and since it
          allocates nothing the memory limit never trips either. Destroying the process is the
          only mechanism that actually ends it.

          android:isolatedProcess="true" is what makes that process boundary a SECURITY
          boundary rather than just a lifecycle one. A plain android:process still runs under
          the app's own UID, so it would inherit INTERNET, the app's private files, and every
          granted permission — meaning a compromised engine could simply open its own sockets
          and ignore the HTTP bridge entirely. An isolated process runs under a dedicated,
          permission-less UID with no filesystem access and no network of its own, so routing
          HTTP through the main process becomes an enforced constraint instead of a convention.

          The two attributes are load-bearing for different reasons and neither substitutes for
          the other: drop isolatedProcess and containment is theatre; drop the separate process
          and a hung script takes the app with it.

          Consequences this imposes on the service, which it is written to satisfy: it may not
          touch the filesystem, may not read app preferences, and may not reach any content
          provider. Everything it needs — the script, its config, its HTTP — arrives over the
          binder.

          android:exported="false" — nothing outside this app may bind. The engine executes
          untrusted third-party code, so an exported binding would hand that execution to any
          app on the device.
        -->
        <service
            android:name="app.otakureader.core.js.engine.JsEngineService"
            android:exported="false"
            android:isolatedProcess="true"
            android:process=":jsengine" />
    </application>

</manifest>
